WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.514
A stimulus is a change in the surroundings, like heat, light or sound. A receptor is a cell, or a group of cells, that detects a stimulus. It then starts a nerve impulse, an electrical signal that travels along nerve cells to the brain or spinal cord.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:35.857
We have five senses: sight, hearing, touch, taste and smell. Each sense organ has receptors for one kind of stimulus. Taste and smell receptors detect chemicals. If a question asks for other senses, do not repeat the one it already gave.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:56.833
Step one: identify the stimulus. Step two: name the receptor in one or two words. In the skin there are three kinds. A temperature receptor detects hot and cold. A pressure receptor detects touch and pressing. A pain receptor detects damage.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:08.604
In the eye, the light receptors are in the retina, the layer at the back. There are two kinds. Rods work in dim light. Cones detect colour.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:30.395
Why are there three types of cone? Light has a wavelength, measured in nanometres, and different wavelengths are different colours. This made-up example graph shows that each cone absorbs a different range: blue, green or red. Together they cover a wide range, and the brain compares them to tell colours apart.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:47.609
Receptors get used to a steady stimulus. Put one hand in hot water and one in ice cold water. Then put both in water at room temperature. The hand from the hot water feels cold. The hand from the cold water feels hot.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:02:06.204
Now a real exam question. This method puts one hand in hot water and one in ice cold water, then both in water at room temperature. The question asks you to name the structure in the skin that detects temperature. One mark.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:16.555
The water at room temperature felt hot to one hand and ice cold to the other. The stimulus is temperature. Now ask: what detects it?

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:23.716
The structure that detects a stimulus is a receptor. So the answer is: temperature receptors in the skin.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:32.111
Here is the answer. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:43.158
A common mistake is to name the whole organ, such as skin or eye. The question wants the structure that does the detecting: the receptor.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:53.239
Another mistake is to call a receptor a sensory neurone. The receptor detects the stimulus. The sensory neurone only carries the impulse onwards.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:06.062
Last, for three cones, do not just say more is better. Say each type absorbs a different range of wavelengths, so together they detect more colours and tell them apart.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:29.706
Your turn. A cook touches a hot pan by mistake. Part a: name the stimulus, and the receptors in her skin that detect it. Part b: some people have only two working types of cone, blue and green. Suggest why they find it hard to tell red and green apart. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:42.817
Part a. The stimulus is heat, a high temperature. It is detected by temperature receptors in the skin. Pain receptors are also stimulated.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:57.713
Part b. With only two types of cone, the range of wavelengths detected is smaller. Without the red cone, red and green light both stimulate mainly the green cones. So the brain cannot tell them apart.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:08.610
So, every time: identify the stimulus. Name the receptor in one or two words. Do not give the whole organ, and do not repeat a sense the question already gave.
